
  [1mB — Session store + tracking facade (recommended).[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m owns storage only. A second small [38;5;153mtracker.js[39m exposes
  [38;5;153mTracker.log(event, detail)[39m, which stamps the current userId automatically. Call sites stop touching [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m for
  user-attributable events and stop touching storage at all.
  [3mBuys:[0m a future form adds two script tags and calls [38;5;153mTracker.log(...)[39m — it cannot forget the userId, and it cannot drift
  on format. Each module answers "what does it do" in one sentence. If tracking ever grows a network sink, only
  [38;5;153mtracker.js[39m changes.
  [3mCosts:[0m two new files instead of one, and a convention to follow.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate — still under ~40 lines total.

  [1mC — One combined module.[0m A single [38;5;153msession.js[39m that owns both the stored id and a [38;5;153mlogWithUser()[39m helper.
  [3mBuys:[0m one file, one global.
  [3mCosts:[0m the module does two things, so "swap the storage" and "change the log format" touch the same file. Cheapest
  now, muddiest later.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  I recommend [1mB[0m. Your stated requirement is reuse across forms that don't exist yet, and B is the only one where a
  future form gets correct tracking by default rather than by remembering. The extra cost is one ~15-line file.

  One thing B doesn't fix, which I'll flag now rather than bury in the spec: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, so there is no server to issue an id. Whatever we build, the id will be locally faked until the real
  call exists. I'll record that in the spec as an explicit assumption with a note on what changes when the API lands,
  rather than writing it as though it's real.
